issues.analysis:
The IEEE journal Author Center policy requires disclosing any AI-generated content in the article, including text, figures, images and code, in the acknowledgments. The disclosure names the AI system and the affected sections. Only editing and grammar help is outside the policy, and disclosure is still recommended even then. The IEEE PSPB Operations Manual (amended 25 June 2026) adds further principles:
- authors check AI output for accuracy and plagiarism;
- AI tools may be used as a source only if independently verified;
- acknowledgment covers text, images, video, audio, graphics and code;
- AI-generated simulation data are labelled;
- code and data release is recommended when AI is the subject of the study.
No IEEE page retrieved says in so many words that AI cannot be an author. PSPB 8.2.1.A reserves authorship for individuals and requires each essential part to be the responsibility of an author, so the handoff's sentence is a paraphrase and should be cited that way. In practice, AI use in NeuroSem already goes beyond 'scaffolding'. In M0, AI agents ran the prior-art sweep, drafted the novelty matrix, screened licences and fixtures, and critiqued the design (AI_USE_LOG.md Entry 001). IEEE Access states it rejects articles containing fabricated content, including nonexistent references. JOSS additionally requires naming the tools and where AI was used, and confirming human review.

According to Claude Code's docs, every session loads the following into context by default:
- CLAUDE.md files from the working directory and every parent directory;
- user-level CLAUDE.md and rules;
- auto memory, which is on by default, stored per repository, with the first 200 lines / 25KB of MEMORY.md loaded.
Non-interactive -p runs load the same context, including project hooks and MCP servers, unless --bare is used. --bare skips hooks, skills, plugins, MCP servers, auto memory and CLAUDE.md, but it does not use subscription login.
This M0 audit shows the problem concretely. It runs from C:/Users/gurra/Continuum, so the Continuum project's auto-memory index was loaded, including feedback memories telling the assistant to act without asking for confirmation and to commit and push at every stopping point. The Continuum project also has a Stop hook that runs git add/commit/push for that repository. Those directives conflict with the handoff's approval gates, and in an agent trial they would be uncontrolled, unrecorded instructions. The NeuroSem repository currently has no .claude directory and no git remote. The final spec asks to record the Claude Code version and model ID, but not the loaded instruction or memory context.

issues.recommendation:
Use a two-stage OSF registration.
Stage 1 (end of M5, before any selection code sees splits), embargoed, Secondary Data template:
- held-out candidate list, split manifest and SHA-256 hashes;
- screening rules and the exact screening outputs allowed to be viewed;
- mutation-family hold-out.
Stage 2 (M8), Simulation Studies or OSF Preregistration template:
- endpoint and analysis plan;
- frozen tolerance and battery hashes;
- crash, equivalent-mutant and undefined-feature rules;
- a deviation policy, e.g. at most one pre-specified redesign, each logged as an OSF Update with a deviations table in the paper.
Keep both registrations embargoed until the agent study is complete. Label M1-M7 as exploratory in the manuscript.

Add to PLAN.md:
- per-milestone time boxes and an overall stop date;
- an M1 gate that counts licensed, valid, independent source families (not cells) available for discovery and held-out sets;
- a split of held-out models by source family, not by cell;
- a precision estimate after the M6 pilot (expected confidence-interval width versus number of base-model clusters) before fixing the full-study size.
Define a minimum viable study in advance, for example fewer mutants per model with the same controls, as the handoff already allows. Record the sag/Ih behaviour gap as a known limitation unless a licensed model is found.
